  <prompt>Build the identity binding that SPA-r2 decided and the owner confirmed on 2026-09-30. A workforce caller binds to a contact through their Entra oid, stored in contact.sprk_externalobjectid. The first sign-in may bind by email only for a home-tenant MEMBER with exactly one match. After that, binding is by oid only. With no match, create a contact keyed by the oid. A person who is both an employee and an invited external user is refused and flagged, because a contact carries one binding. In the same task, link every licensed systemuser to its contact the same way, and maintain sprk_primarycontact. Assigned-To auto-grants, the No Access List for internal users, and briefing "assigned to" matching all depend on that link, and today only 1 of 8 active dev users has it.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer and identity engineer. Knows Entra token claims (oid, tid, acct, idtyp, email, preferred_username), Dataverse alternate keys and field-level security, and the Spaarke CIAM and workforce resolution planes (ADR-028 A1/A2). Follows ADRs strictly.</role>

  <goal>An employee of the configured customer workforce tenant (a member, acct=0) with no Power Apps licence (Type 2) resolves to exactly one contact bound by oid, and no longer gets 403 principal_not_resolved in dev. Every active licensed systemuser either has a verified systemuser↔contact link (contact.sprk_externalobjectid = systemuser.azureactivedirectoryobjectid, and systemuser.sprk_primarycontact pointing at that contact) or carries a durable, operator-visible collision flag. No code path queries contact.azureactivedirectoryobjectid. No path resolves or binds a contact on an unguarded email match: that covers the workforce fallback, the systemuser email fallback in AccessibleRecordSetService, the CIAM first-login fallback and the invite resolve-or-create. SPA-r2 task 023 is recorded as delivered by this task.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>WHO THIS IS FOR. A Type-2 user is a customer employee without a Power Apps licence who signs in with company SSO. They use SPA and Teams only, never MDA. Four earlier decisions require this user: teams-app-r1 D11 "Option B", SPA-r2 Legal Front Door, UAC-r2 B-4 and ADR-028 A2. The owner's model is that internal workers are matched automatically, external users arrive by invitation, and contact-based users get SPA/Teams only.

WHAT IS BROKEN TODAY. Verified 2026-09-30 against source and against live spaarkedev1.
1. THE WORKFORCE CONTACT-ONLY BRANCH QUERIES A COLUMN THAT DOES NOT EXIST IN DEV. In `WorkforcePrincipalResolver.cs:153-202` the branch calls `IdentityNormalizationService.TryResolveContactByWorkforceIdentityAsync` (`IdentityNormalizationService.cs:242-303`).
   - The oid lookup `TryResolveContactIdAsync` (`:535-577`) filters `contact.azureactivedirectoryobjectid` (`:547-550`).
   - The email query (`:590-654`) SELECTS the same column (`:598`).
   - The spaarkedev1 contact entity has no such attribute. Both queries throw and are caught as null (`:568-576`, `:646-653`), and `:273-281` then denies. Every Type-2 employee therefore gets 403.
   - Where the column does exist but is empty (nothing in src writes it; the only write of azureactivedirectoryobjectid is to systemuser, at `RegistrationDataverseService.cs:372`), the email match is a hijack path. Two reasons: `ExtractVerifiedEmail` (`WorkforcePrincipalResolver.cs:220-228`) verifies nothing, and task 013's guard can only deny a contact bound to a different oid, which never exists.
2. THE INERT-GUARD WARNING IS WRONG. `GuardInertNoBindingColumn` (`IdentityNormalizationService.cs:353`, emitted at `:620-635`) can only fire when the query SUCCEEDS. When the column is absent, the query throws first, so the warning never fires in exactly the environment its comment describes.
3. A THIRD READER OF THE ABSENT COLUMN. `Services/Ai/Context/CallerContactResolver.cs:112-118` resolves "assign it to me" through `contact.azureactivedirectoryobjectid`. In dev it returns lookup-failed for everyone. The systemuser fallback at `IdentityNormalizationService.cs:146-150` uses the same broken lookup.
4. THE LICENSED USER'S CONTACT LINK IS MOSTLY MISSING. `IdentityNormalizationService.ResolveAsync` derives a systemuser's contact from `systemuser.sprk_primarycontact` (`:140-150`, read at `:213`/`:222`). `MembershipResolverService.cs:868-889` silently skips Contact-typed membership descriptors (assignedAttorney, assignedParalegal, assignedTo*) when there is no contact and only logs `member_skipped`. Several consumers depend on `sprk_primarycontact`: Assigned-To access (C9, which the owner calls "one of the core reasons for the UAC"), the No Access List for internal users (Q4), briefing "assigned to" matching (Q8, `useInlineTodoCreate.ts:221-237`), and the internal-vs-external badge (`TrackingFieldTrio/index.ts:966-969`).
5. THE SYSTEMUSER EMAIL FALLBACK BYPASSES EVERY GUARD. With no linked contact, `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1022-1026` calls `ResolveExternalContactAsync(oid: null, email: principal.Email)`. That method returns an UNBOUND contact on an email match with `$top=1`, no ambiguity check and no binding (`ExternalParticipationService.cs:333-347`), so a licensed user inherits the grants of any unbound contact that carries their email. `IIdentityNormalizationService.cs:73-75` already records this.
6. CIAM FALLBACK WEAKNESSES. `ExternalParticipationService.cs`:
   - `ResolveContactRowByEmailAsync` uses `$top=1` with no ambiguity check (`:423`).
   - `ResolveContactByOidAsync` uses `$top=1`, so two contacts carrying one oid pick one silently (`:375`).
   - The oid comparison is a string compare (`:352`), not a parsed-Guid compare.
   - Neither query filters `statecode`.
   - `BindOidToContactAsync` (`:460-490`) writes any oid onto an unbound contact.
   - A failed oid read is indistinguishable from "no contact": `ResolveContactByOidAsync` returns null on a non-success status or an exception (`:384-397`), and `ResolveExternalContactAsync` then falls through to the email fallback (`:327-348`). The email path can then bind the same oid onto a SECOND contact. The workforce oid lookup has the same collapse (`IdentityNormalizationService.cs:564-576`).
   - `ResolveContactByEmailAsync` (`:404-408`) is public, unguarded (`$top=1`) and has zero callers in src or tests.
   - The only production caller of the CIAM resolution is `CiamContactPrincipalStrategy` (`CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:339-373`). It collapses every non-resolution into one deny code (`sdap.access.deny.contact_not_found`, `:371-372`), so an ambiguity or collision deny is not distinguishable in the audit trail.
7. INVITE COLLISION IS SILENT. `InviteExternalUserEndpoint.ResolveOrCreateContactAsync` (`:173-215`) resolves by email with `top: 1`. `ProvisionAsync` (`:127-135`) returns "AlreadyProvisioned" for ANY bound contact, and it is shared with `/invite-and-grant` (`InviteAndGrantExternalUserEndpoint.cs:97`). After this task a bound contact may carry a WORKFORCE oid, so inviting an employee's email would silently skip creating a CIAM account, and the external person could never sign in.
8. THE LINK IS CACHED. `IdentityNormalizationService.ResolveAsync` caches `PersonIdentity` for 10 minutes (`:55`, written at `:174`) and has no invalidation. An inline link written during a request would therefore not take effect for up to 10 minutes unless the cache entry is replaced.
9. "HOME TENANT" IS NOT ALWAYS AzureAd:TenantId. D-13 (`notes/D-13-per-customer-bff-app-registration.md` §1): in Model 1 the per-customer BFF app registration lives in SPAARKE's tenant, so a Model-1 customer's employees carry the CUSTOMER's `tid`, not `AzureAd:TenantId`. In Model 2 the registration is in the customer's tenant and the two coincide. A member test keyed only on `AzureAd:TenantId` would refuse every Model-1 Type-2 employee and would auto-bind Spaarke's own staff into a customer's environment. The existing `TenantRouting:Tenants[].Tid` map (`Infrastructure/Routing/TenantEnvironmentRoutingOptions.cs:57-99`) names customer workforce tenants, but it is not configured in `appsettings.template.json`. The multitenant app registration itself (prerequisite E-6) is still open.
10. `idtyp` IS OPTIONAL AND EXPECTED ABSENT (`notes/task-081-caller-classification.md:38`). The repo's caller-kind classifier is `CallerIdentity.FromPrincipal` (`src/server/shared/Spaarke.Core/Auth/CallerIdentity.cs:229`). It honours `idtyp` when it is present and otherwise uses `scp` and `sub`==`oid`.

OUT OF SCOPE, stated so that nobody "fixes" it by mistake: `ICommunicationDataverseService.QueryContactByEmailAsync` (`src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse/DataverseServiceClientImpl.cs:3065-3081`, used by communication participant correlation) matches email participants to contacts for indexing. It is not a caller-identity or binding path.

LIVE DATA (spaarkedev1, 2026-09-30):
- 8 active interactive systemusers.
- 1 has `sprk_primarycontact`: ralph.schroeder@spaarke.com → contact 8e9918a9. That contact's `sprk_externalobjectid` is 6a9fa229…, NOT the user's oid c74ac1af…
- 3 users have an email-matched contact already bound to a different (CIAM-written) oid: ralph.schroeder@spaarke.com, eyal.iffergan@spaarke.com and ralph.schroeder@hotmail.com. That is the collision case, live.
- 1 user (testuser1@spaarke.com) has an UNBOUND email-matched contact, so it is a bind candidate.
- 4 users (final.test, demo, jake.schroeder and e2e.test2 @demo.spaarke.com) have no contact, so each needs one created.
- 6 contacts in total carry `sprk_externalobjectid`, and every existing writer is the CIAM path.
- `sprk_externalobjectid` is a TEXT column, so it cannot say which plane wrote a value.

WHAT SPA-r2 ALREADY DECIDED. spaarke-SPA-external-access-platform-r2 spec FR-11 and assumption spec.md:224 widen `sprk_externalobjectid` to hold a CIAM OR a workforce oid (one field for both planes; oids are globally unique). Task 023 (`projects/spaarke-SPA-external-access-platform-r2/tasks/023-lazy-contact-attribution.poml`, status not-started, no code exists; a grep for ContactAttribution finds nothing) was the build. Its escalation trigger named the cross-plane collision, and the owner has now answered it: refuse and flag.</background>
