  <constraints>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 item 4 (BINDING)">Scope: every workforce and CIAM contact binding.
- The binding key is the Entra oid, stored in `contact.sprk_externalobjectid`. There is ONE field for both planes. Do not add a second oid column.
- An email bind happens ONLY at first sign-in, ONLY with exactly one active matching contact that is unbound, and ONLY for the eligible caller of each plane. On the workforce plane that is a MEMBER of the configured customer workforce tenant. The CIAM and systemuser rules are in the next constraints.
- After a contact is bound, resolution is by oid only, and email is never consulted again for that caller.
- On the WORKFORCE plane only, when nothing matches, create a contact keyed by the oid at first sign-in. This is settled by owner round 2 item 4 ("Otherwise create the contact keyed by oid") and Q3, and it supersedes SPA-r2 FR-11's "no Contact merely by having access" wording (step 10 records this). It is not an open question.
- A contact carries one binding. A person who is both an employee and an invited external user is REFUSED and FLAGGED, never merged and never re-bound.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 item 4 + ADR-003 + D-13">Scope: the member-or-guest decision on the WORKFORCE plane (token callers).
- A member is a caller whose `tid` is a customer workforce tenant served by this deployment AND whose `acct` claim is 0.
- The customer workforce tenant is NOT `AzureAd:TenantId` by definition. It coincides with it in Model 2, but not in Model 1 (background item 9). Read it from one explicit, per-deployment configuration source: the existing `TenantRouting:Tenants[].Tid` map if execution confirms it is the authoritative list, otherwise a single new setting documented in the customer deployment guide. An empty or missing value denies every email bind and creation (fail closed). It never defaults to `AzureAd:TenantId`. See the escalation for the choice of source.
- A guest (`acct` = 1), a caller from any other tenant (including the app registration's own tenant in Model 1 when that is not the customer tenant), or a token with no `acct` claim gets NO email bind and NO contact creation. Such a caller resolves only through an existing oid binding and is otherwise denied.
- If `acct` is absent, fail closed. Never infer member status from the email domain or from a `#EXT#` UPN.
- App-only tokens are never bound. Classify with `CallerIdentity.FromPrincipal` (`Spaarke.Core/Auth/CallerIdentity.cs:229`), not with `idtyp` alone, because `idtyp` is optional and expected absent.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-028 A1 + owner model (externals arrive by invitation)">Scope: the CIAM plane (`ExternalParticipationService`, `CiamContactPrincipalStrategy`).
- The CIAM plane NEVER creates a contact. External users arrive by invitation, and the invite already writes the oid (`InviteExternalUserEndpoint.cs:141-145`).
- The CIAM first-login email bind is RETAINED as the repair path for an invite whose oid write failed after the CIAM account was created (`ProvisionAsync` creates the account at `:138` and writes the oid at `:141-145`). It binds only onto exactly one ACTIVE contact that is unbound, is not marked with the Workforce plane, and is not referenced by any systemuser's `sprk_primarycontact`. Anything else is a collision: deny and flag.
- The `acct` member test does not apply to CIAM local accounts. The CIAM plane is identified as today, by `tid` equal to `Ciam:TenantId` (`CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:302-309`).</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 item 4 + D-1">Scope: licensed systemusers linked WITHOUT a token (the reconciliation job and `RegistrationDataverseService`).
- The oid is `systemuser.azureactivedirectoryobjectid` (directory-synced). The email is `systemuser.internalemailaddress` (directory-synced), never a client value.
- The member-or-guest rule still applies. A systemuser whose `domainname` marks a B2B guest (`#EXT#`) gets no email bind: create-by-oid when no active contact carries its email; otherwise flag it and write nothing.
- Only enabled, interactive users are processed (`isdisabled` = false; `accessmode` excludes non-interactive and application users; `applicationid` null).</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 item 4">Scope: collisions.
- A collision is any of: an email match bound to a different oid; an oid carried by more than one contact; a licensed user whose `sprk_primarycontact` points at a contact bound to a different oid; or an invite whose email matches a workforce-bound contact or a contact referenced by a systemuser's `sprk_primarycontact`.
- Every collision produces a deny or refusal PLUS a durable flag that an operator can see in Dataverse. A log line alone does not count as a flag.
- The flag records the colliding identity (oid and plane) and the time. Writing it is idempotent: a repeated collision by the same identity does not write again, so a caller retrying a denied sign-in cannot turn the deny path into a stream of Dataverse writes.
- An operator (System Administrator) resolves a collision in Dataverse by the documented procedure. The next reconciliation run re-evaluates the contact and clears the flag once the collision no longer holds. The procedure is written in the notes and in `SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md`.
- The API response carries a message and a distinct reason code, not a bare 500.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">Scope: inactive contacts. An oid carried by an INACTIVE contact denies, with its own deny code. It never falls through to the email bind and never creates a replacement contact. Deactivating a contact is how an operator removes a person, and auto-creation must not undo that.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">Scope: every oid lookup on both planes. A lookup has three outcomes: found, not found and could-not-read. Could-not-read (a non-success status, an exception or a timeout) DENIES. It does not fall through to the email path or to creation. Today both planes collapse it into "not found" (background item 6).</constraint>
    <constraint source="project (ADR-009 cache)">Scope: the `PersonIdentity` cache. A bind or link written during a request takes effect on that request, either by returning the new identity directly or by replacing the cache entry for that systemuser. It must not wait out the 10-minute TTL.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project (live data)">Scope: licensed systemusers. NEVER clear or re-point an existing `sprk_primarycontact` automatically. A licensed user whose existing link collides is flagged and left exactly as it is until the owner decides (see escalation). Removing Ralph's link in dev, for example, would silently remove his Assigned-To access.</constraint>
    <constraint source="SPA-r2 FR-11 + owner round 2">Scope: contact creation.
- Creation is idempotent by oid: exactly one contact per oid, including when two first sign-ins race.
- The created contact carries the oid, first and last name and email from the token, and no grants.
- Creating a contact grants no access. Access still comes only from Dataverse (systemusers) or explicit grants (contacts), as C9 decided.</constraint>
    <constraint source="D-1 / DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE WP-1, WP-5">Scope: where the link is maintained.
- Every write of the binding and of `sprk_primarycontact` happens in the BFF. No plugin.
- Systemusers are created outside the product (Entra/PPAC sync), so an ADR-036 `IScheduledJob` reconciliation keeps the link, placed per ADR-052. It is needed because an internal user named in an Assigned-To field needs the link even if they never sign in to the BFF.
- Inline binding at first resolution and at `RegistrationDataverseService.CreateSystemUserAsync` covers the fast path. No new hand-rolled timer `BackgroundService`.
- `CreateSystemUserAsync` writes the link to the SAME environment it created the systemuser in (`targetDataverseUrl`).
- The job copies the safety convention of `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob`. Linking a systemuser to an existing contact hands that user the contact's grants, which is the same class of write as that job's R1. So: an `AddScheduledJob` registration; a writes switch named positively, where absent, empty or unparseable means report-only; a before-state log line for every row it would change; a failed scan recorded as a failed run (never as "nothing to reconcile"); and no throw from `ExecuteAsync`.
- The switch is a rollout guard, not a deferral. In dev the live gate runs report-only, the report is reviewed, and then writes are enabled.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project (security, discovered 2026-09-30)">Scope: `contact.sprk_externalobjectid` and `systemuser.sprk_primarycontact`. Once these decide whose grants a caller inherits, anyone with Write on contact could put their own oid on a contact that holds grants. Lock both with field-level security in the same way owner decision round 2 item 2 locks `sprk_issecure`: only the BFF app user can write.
- READ must stay available to every user who reads the fields today, because a secured column is hidden from anyone without a Read profile. The live client reads are `useInlineTodoCreate.ts:232` (systemuser `_sprk_primarycontact_value`) and `TrackingFieldTrio/index.ts:969` (a filter on `_sprk_primarycontact_value`). Both run as the signed-in user. So Read goes to all users, and Create and Update go only to the write profile.
- The BFF application user is added to the write profile EXPLICITLY. Do not rely on its current System Administrator role, which D-13 per-customer app users may not carry.
- Verify the current state live first (the session-27 check found 0 `fieldpermission` rows for the access fields).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-028">Scope: all changed code. Keep the resolver broker-only: claims are read from the validated token and Dataverse is queried app-only. No OBO or Graph call with the caller token. ADR-028 A4: add no new `.WithClientSecret(...)`. Reuse the existing `IDataverseService` / `ExternalParticipationService` token paths.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">Scope: every resolution path.
- If the binding state cannot be read, deny.
- An ambiguous match is denied, never resolved to the first row.
- Compare oids as parsed `Guid`s, never as strings, and write them in "D" (lowercase, hyphenated) format.
- Only active contacts (statecode 0) resolve or bind.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: tests.
- Decisions are pure functions asserted directly.
- No `Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;`, no DI-registration tests, no constructor null-check tests.
- Tests go at the KEEP paths `tests/integration/auth/**` and `tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/**`.
- Exercising internals through `InternalsVisibleTo` is an ADR-038 §7 B8 deviation that task 013 left pending (`IdentityNormalizationService.cs:315-321`). Surface it as a CLAUDE.md §6.5 path A/B in the PR. Do not copy it silently.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner directive">Scope: live verification. No Dataverse test runs in CI. The live proof is a MANUAL gate that uses the existing non-admin dev test users in their current BUs. Do not propose relocating users.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this task's PR.
- State the placement decision, citing `.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md`.
- Measure the publish-size delta against a FRESH build of master, with both sides in fresh short-path worktrees, using PowerShell `Compress-Archive` and equal file counts. The ceiling is 60 MB.
- Report no new HIGH CVE.
- Tests are updated for every changed service.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner directive">Scope: this whole task. No scope cuts and nothing deferred. Scope discovered during execution is added to this task, not pushed off.</constraint>
  </constraints>

