  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Load the knowledge files and declare FULL rigor. Re-verify every anchor in the background against current source, and re-run the live systemuser and contact queries. Record the 2026-09-30 baseline counts (1 linked, 3 collisions, 1 bind candidate, 4 to create) in notes/task-141-identity-binding.md.</step>
    <step order="1">Design the single binding decision as a pure function. Its inputs:
- the plane (Workforce token, CIAM token, or Systemuser without a token);
- the identity facts (oid; for a Workforce token also tid against the configured customer workforce tenant(s), acct, the CallerIdentity kind and the email claim; for a Systemuser the directory-synced email and the guest marker);
- the oid lookup result (a three-state value: rows, or could-not-read), including each row's statecode;
- the email lookup rows, each with its binding, plane marker, statecode and whether any systemuser's `sprk_primarycontact` references it.
Its outcomes are ResolveByOid / BindByEmail / CreateByOid / Deny(reason) / Collision(reason).
Write the decision table in the notes before coding. Include every negative case named in the acceptance criteria.
Resolve the customer-tenant configuration source (see escalation) before coding the member test.</step>
    <step order="2">Schema, applied to dev in this order (the sequence is binding):
(a) Confirm `sprk_externalobjectid` carries only well-formed oids today, then normalise them to "D" format.
(b) Enforce one contact per oid. Use a Dataverse alternate key if the platform accepts it on a column that is null for unbound contacts; otherwise handle create-then-requery for races. Record which approach was used and why.
(c) Add the plane and collision marker the refusals need (see justification), and backfill the 6 existing bindings as External, because every current writer is CIAM.
(d) Apply field-level security to `contact.sprk_externalobjectid` and `systemuser.sprk_primarycontact`.
Export the changes into the owning solution so they deploy to other environments.</step>
    <step order="3">Workforce plane.
- Replace every query of `contact.azureactivedirectoryobjectid` (`IdentityNormalizationService.cs:541-550`, `:596-599`, `CallerContactResolver.cs:112-118`) with the `sprk_externalobjectid` binding. Route the systemuser fallback (`:146-150`) through it as well.
- Wire the decision into `TryResolveContactByWorkforceIdentityAsync` / `WorkforcePrincipalResolver`, including the member test and the create-by-oid write.
- Make the oid lookup three-state. Could-not-read denies (`TryResolveContactIdAsync`, `:535-577`, currently returns null on an exception).
- Rewrite or retire `GuardInertNoBindingColumn` so that any warning which remains fires only on a condition that really means the guard cannot discriminate, with an accurate comment. A missing binding column must surface as its own deny code with an error log, not a silent null.</step>
    <step order="4">Licensed-user link.
- Inline: when a systemuser resolves with no linked contact, apply the same decision keyed by the systemuser's own oid, then set `sprk_primarycontact`. Make the new link effective on the same request (the cache constraint).
- `RegistrationDataverseService.CreateSystemUserAsync`: link or create at creation time, in the target environment.
- Reconciliation: an ADR-036 `IScheduledJob` walks enabled interactive systemusers, links or creates, flags collisions, clears flags whose collision no longer holds, and never re-points an existing link. It is idempotent, pages through users, is cheap when nothing has changed, and reports counts per outcome. It follows the `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob` safety convention.
- Remove the email fallback at `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1022-1026`. A licensed user's contact comes only from the link.</step>
    <step order="5">CIAM plane (`ExternalParticipationService.cs`):
- The email fallback reads two rows and denies ambiguity.
- The oid lookup reads two rows and denies ambiguity.
- Compare oids as parsed Guids.
- Filter to active contacts.
- The oid lookup is three-state. Could-not-read denies and does not fall through to the email fallback.
- `BindOidToContactAsync` refuses a contact that is already bound (either plane), is marked Workforce, or is referenced by a systemuser's `sprk_primarycontact`. It records the CIAM plane on a successful bind. A failed bind write is not silently "resolved anyway": decide it in the decision table and record the choice.
- The CIAM plane never creates a contact.
- Delete `ResolveContactByEmailAsync` (`:404-408`, zero callers), so no unguarded `$top=1` email lookup remains.
- `CiamContactPrincipalStrategy` (`CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:362-373`) returns the decision's distinct deny code (ambiguity, collision, unreadable, inactive) instead of the single `contact_not_found`.
Use the same pure decision as the workforce plane, with each plane passing its own facts.</step>
    <step order="6">Invite path (`InviteExternalUserEndpoint.cs:121-135,173-215`, shared with `InviteAndGrantExternalUserEndpoint.cs:97`):
- Resolve by email, reading two rows of ACTIVE contacts.
- A contact bound on the WORKFORCE plane, or referenced by any systemuser's `sprk_primarycontact`, is refused and flagged. The refusal is HTTP 409 with a ProblemDetails message and a distinct reason code, instead of "AlreadyProvisioned". Both `/invite` and `/invite-and-grant` return it, and `/invite-and-grant` writes no grant.
- A contact bound on the CIAM plane keeps today's idempotent behaviour.
- An ambiguous email is refused (409, with a message and its own reason code).</step>
    <step order="7">Configuration and app registration.
- Add the `acct` optional claim to the BFF access-token configuration in `scripts/Register-EntraAppRegistrations.ps1`. Apply it to the dev BFF registration.
- Add the customer-workforce-tenant setting chosen in step 1 to `appsettings.template.json` and to the dev configuration.
- Document both as per-customer app-registration and deployment requirements (D-13) in `SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md` (§6.5 / §7.3), the authoritative guide. `auth-deployment-setup.md` is a retired stub. Cover Model 1, where the tenant differs from `AzureAd:TenantId`.</step>
    <step order="8">Tests at the KEEP paths:
- The full decision table.
- Resolver wiring (the contact-only branch no longer denies when a binding exists).
- CallerContactResolver resolves by the binding.
- The reconciliation job's decision over row sets.
- The CIAM ambiguity, Guid-compare, no-create and could-not-read cases.
- The invite refusal on both `/invite` and `/invite-and-grant`.
- The Model-1 tenant case and the app-only-token-without-`idtyp` case.
- A source guard test that fails if any query against the contact entity names `azureactivedirectoryobjectid` again. Prove it bites by seeding one.
Rewrite the inert-guard tests (`WorkforceEmailNoHijackTests.cs:588-620`) and the residual note (`:795-814`) to match the new behaviour.</step>
    <step order="9">Deploy the BFF to dev (bff-deploy skill). Run the reconciliation once in report-only mode and review the report against the step-0 baseline. Then enable writes in dev and run it again. Execute the manual live gate in the acceptance criteria. Record the before and after counts and each flagged collision in the notes.</step>
    <step order="10">Close the paper trail:
- Mark `projects/spaarke-SPA-external-access-platform-r2/tasks/023-lazy-contact-attribution.poml` as completed with a note "delivered by unified-access-control-r2 task 141". Update SPA-r2 `TASK-INDEX.md:41`.
- Record in that project's notes that creating a contact at first member sign-in supersedes FR-11's "no Contact merely by access" wording, per owner round 2. The P3 intake-submit requester wiring stays with SPA-r2 as their consumer of this mechanism.
- Remove the "do not mark A-18 closed" residual at `IIdentityNormalizationService.cs:50-75` and point the re-opened task 013 at this task as the closure of A-18.</step>
    <step order="11">Run the full BFF unit suite, integration auth tests and NetArchTest. Measure publish size per CLAUDE.md §10. Update this project's TASK-INDEX status to `✅ [done]` and document deviations in notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>OWNER DECISION on the handling of COLLISION rows for licensed users. It blocks only those rows, not the rest of step 4.
Dev already has 3 licensed users whose email-matched contact carries a different (CIAM) oid, and one of them (ralph.schroeder@spaarke.com) already has `sprk_primarycontact` pointing at that contact. The rule is "refuse and flag". Options:
(a) flag, with no new link, until an operator resolves it;
(b) keep or create a `sprk_primarycontact` link for internal matching while the workforce oid stays unbound;
(c) create a new, separate contact for the workforce identity.
RECOMMENDATION: (a), which is also the default until answered. It is the owner's literal rule and it never lets one contact carry two planes' access. It costs no existing access, because the constraint already forbids clearing or re-pointing an existing `sprk_primarycontact` (Ralph's Assigned-To access continues). The dev collisions are test artefacts (internal addresses invited as externals), and an operator resolves each one by deciding which identity keeps the contact.
Rejected: (b) mixes a CIAM-bound contact's grants into an internal user's accessible set through `AccessibleRecordSetService`'s contact-grant term. (c) creates duplicate contacts for one person, so Assigned-To fields that already name the old contact stop matching.</trigger>
    <trigger>CONFIGURATION SOURCE for "customer workforce tenant", needed at step 1. If `TenantRouting:Tenants[]` (`TenantEnvironmentRoutingOptions.cs`) is confirmed as the authoritative per-deployment list of customer workforce tenants, use it. If it is not, or if it conflicts with the D-13 Model-1 layout, stop and ask.
RECOMMENDATION: one explicit per-deployment setting (a list of customer workforce tenant ids). It is written by provisioning, empty means deny, and it never defaults to `AzureAd:TenantId`.
Do NOT hard-code `AzureAd:TenantId`. In Model 1 that would refuse every customer employee and bind Spaarke's own staff instead (background item 9).</trigger>
    <trigger>If the `acct` optional claim cannot be issued on the workforce token for this app registration, or cannot be relied on for Teams SSO tokens, STOP. The member test is the security property of the email bind, and replacing it with a domain or UPN heuristic is not an implementation detail. There is no safe fallback. Without `acct`, the constraint already denies both the email bind and creation. Report the Microsoft-documented alternatives, with sources, and let the owner choose. Never infer membership.</trigger>
    <trigger>If field-level security on `sprk_primarycontact` or `sprk_externalobjectid` breaks an existing client write, STOP rather than drop the lock. A known example would be a form or PCF that sets `sprk_primarycontact`; a 2026-09-30 grep of src found no client writer, only the reads listed in the FLS constraint. Such writes must move behind a BFF endpoint, as for `sprk_issecure` (owner round 2 item 2). Report the writer and the proposed endpoint before building it.</trigger>
    <trigger>If a ruling requires relaxing any existing alternate key, or making a record's TargetEntity required, STOP. Both are forbidden by owner directive.</trigger>
    <trigger>If no unlicensed home-tenant MEMBER test account exists in the dev Entra tenant for the live gate, ask the owner for one. Do not relocate an existing user or make one a guest.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>Grep, 2026-09-30:
- The binding machinery that exists is CIAM-only and private: `ExternalParticipationService.BindOidToContactAsync` (`:460`) and the invite `ResolveOrCreateContactAsync` (`InviteExternalUserEndpoint.cs:173`, email-keyed, top 1).
- The SPA-r2 `ContactAttributionService` was never built (a grep for ContactAttribution or EnsureRequesterContact returns nothing).
- No code writes or reconciles `systemuser.sprk_primarycontact`. A grep of src finds only reads: `IdentityNormalizationService.cs:213,222`, `useInlineTodoCreate.ts:232` and `TrackingFieldTrio/index.ts:969`.
- Existing ADR-036 jobs, grep `: IScheduledJob`: `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob` (grant expiry stamp, inactive-organization and ended-membership deactivation), `MembershipReconciliationJob` (the `sprk_userentityassociation` junction), `GrantExpiryReminderJob` and `PlaybookSchedulerJob`. None touches identity binding.
- No contact column records which plane wrote `sprk_externalobjectid`, and none records a binding collision (a grep for sprk_externalidentity, sprk_identityplane, sprk_identitysource, or identity/binding conflict finds nothing relevant).</existing>
    <extension>Extend first:
- The decision grows out of `DecideWorkforceEmailMatch` and `ReadOidBinding`.
- The CIAM and invite paths call it instead of carrying their own copies.
- Resolution stays in `IdentityNormalizationService` and `ExternalParticipationService`.
Three things are genuinely new:
(1) One small binding writer (bind or create by oid, set `sprk_primarycontact`, raise the collision flag), shared by the workforce resolver, registration, the reconciliation job and the CIAM bind. `IdentityNormalizationService` is a read-only resolver with a 10-minute cache, and giving it Dataverse writes adds a second reason to change (CLAUDE.md §11.5). If execution shows the writer fits cohesively in an existing class, extend that class instead and say so in the PR.
(2) One ADR-036 `IScheduledJob` for reconciliation. It is not folded into `ExternalAccessReconciliationJob`. That job's single reason to change is the lifecycle of grant and membership rows, and it scans `sprk_externalrecordaccess` / `sprk_contactorganization`. This job scans systemusers and contacts for identity binding, which is a different reason to change (CLAUDE.md §11.5). It copies that job's safety convention instead. If execution finds the two share a scan or a schedule cohesively, extend the existing job and say so in the PR.
(3) Schema: a plane/collision choice column on contact (or the smallest equivalent), plus a uniqueness guarantee on `sprk_externalobjectid`.
(4) Configuration, only if `TenantRouting:Tenants[]` is not confirmed as the source: one per-deployment list of customer workforce tenant ids (see escalation).</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Concrete failures:
- Every Type-2 employee is denied 403 principal_not_resolved in dev today.
- 7 of 8 licensed dev users have no contact link, so Assigned-To access (C9), the No Access List for internal users (Q4) and briefing matching (Q8) silently skip them (`MembershipResolverService.cs:868-889`, member_skipped).
- `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:1022-1026` hands a licensed user the grants of any unbound contact that carries their email.
- Without a plane marker, `ProvisionAsync` (`:127-135`) answers "AlreadyProvisioned" when an employee is invited as an external, so the invitee never gets a CIAM account and nobody is told.
- Without a uniqueness guarantee, two concurrent first sign-ins create two contacts for one oid, and the next oid lookup is ambiguous and denies.
- Without an explicit customer-workforce-tenant source, the member test either refuses every Model-1 customer employee or binds Spaarke's own staff into a customer environment.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

