  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a member (tid in the configured customer workforce tenant(s), acct=0) whose oid is already bound to an active contact, the caller resolves to that contact by oid, and email is not consulted.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a member with no oid binding and exactly one active, unbound contact matching their email (not marked with another plane, and not referenced by a systemuser's `sprk_primarycontact` of a different oid), the first sign-in binds the oid to that contact (in "D" format, plane Workforce) and resolves to it. The next sign-in resolves by oid, even if the email claim then differs.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a member with no oid binding and no email match, exactly one contact keyed by the oid is created and resolved. Two concurrent first sign-ins still leave exactly one contact for that oid. The created contact holds no grants.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative (collision): an email match on a contact bound to a different oid, on either plane, is denied, writes no binding, creates no contact and raises a durable collision flag that records the colliding oid and plane. A repeated identical collision writes no second flag.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative (ambiguity): two or more active contacts carrying the email, or two contacts carrying the oid, are denied on both planes. No first-row pick, no create, and a flag is raised.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative (authorization, workforce): a guest (acct=1), a caller whose tid is not a configured customer workforce tenant, a token with no acct claim, or an app-only token as classified by `CallerIdentity.FromPrincipal` (including one with no `idtyp` claim) gets no email bind and no contact creation. It resolves only through an existing oid binding and is otherwise denied with a deny code.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative (authorization, Model 1): with the customer workforce tenant configured as T and `AzureAd:TenantId` set to a different tenant S, an acct=0 member of S gets no email bind and no creation, and an acct=0 member of T does. With the customer-tenant setting empty, nobody gets an email bind or creation.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: an unreadable binding (malformed or all-zero value) or a Dataverse read failure on either the oid or the email lookup denies on both planes. It never resolves, never binds, never falls through from a failed oid read to the email path, and never creates.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative (inactive): an oid carried only by an INACTIVE contact denies with its own deny code. It does not email-bind another contact and does not create a replacement. An inactive contact is never an email-bind target.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">CIAM never creates a contact, and the CIAM email bind refuses a contact marked Workforce or referenced by a systemuser's `sprk_primarycontact`. `CiamContactPrincipalStrategy` returns the distinct deny code for ambiguity, collision, unreadable and inactive, not the single `contact_not_found`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A bind or link written during a request is reflected in that request's resolved identity (contact id present) without waiting for the 10-minute `PersonIdentity` cache TTL.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No query against the contact entity names `azureactivedirectoryobjectid` anywhere in src. A guard test fails if one is added, and it was shown to bite by seeding one.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The inert-guard warning is either gone or fires only on a condition that really disables the check, and its comment is accurate. A missing binding column produces its own deny code and an error log.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`AccessibleRecordSetService` no longer resolves a licensed user's contact by email. The only source is the systemuser↔contact link.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">CIAM: both lookups read two rows and deny ambiguity, oids are compared as parsed Guids, only active contacts resolve, and a bind onto an already-bound contact is refused. `ResolveContactByEmailAsync` no longer exists.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Invite: on both `/invite` and `/invite-and-grant`, an email matching a WORKFORCE-bound contact, or a contact referenced by a systemuser's `sprk_primarycontact`, is refused with HTTP 409, a ProblemDetails message and a distinct reason code, and is flagged. No CIAM account is created and no grant is written. It is not answered "AlreadyProvisioned". A CIAM-bound contact keeps today's idempotent response. An ambiguous email is refused (409, with a message). No path returns a bare 500.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Reconciliation decision: over a row set shaped like the 2026-09-30 dev data, the job links the unbound candidate, creates the missing contacts, flags the 3 collisions, leaves the existing `sprk_primarycontact` untouched, and on a second run changes nothing. A guest systemuser (`#EXT#` in `domainname`) is never email-bound. A flag whose collision no longer holds is cleared.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Reconciliation wiring: the job is registered through the ADR-036 `AddScheduledJob` path with a cron. Its writes switch defaults to report-only when the value is absent, empty or unparseable. In report-only mode it logs the before-state and the intended change for every row and writes nothing. A failed scan ends the run as `Success=false`, never as zero drift.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`RegistrationDataverseService.CreateSystemUserAsync` links or creates the contact in the same `targetDataverseUrl` environment as the systemuser, never in the default environment.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`CallerContactResolver` ("assign it to me") resolves through the binding for a linked user and returns an honest unresolved result otherwise.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Authorization: a non-admin user without the write profile cannot create or update `contact.sprk_externalobjectid` or `systemuser.sprk_primarycontact`. The BFF application user can, through its explicit membership of the write profile. A non-admin user can still READ both fields.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="false">MANUAL LIVE GATE (dev), using existing test users in their current BUs:
(1) An unlicensed member of the configured customer workforce tenant signs in to Teams or SPA and resolves (no 403). One contact is bound or created. The second sign-in resolves by oid (App Insights shows the oid path).
(2) A guest or no-acct token is not bound.
(3) The 3 known collision emails are refused and flagged, and the refusal is visible to an operator in Dataverse.
(4) The reconciliation job runs first in report-only mode (it writes nothing, and its report is recorded), then with writes enabled. After that, every enabled interactive systemuser is either linked (`sprk_primarycontact` set and `contact.sprk_externalobjectid` equal to the systemuser oid) or flagged. Before and after counts are recorded.
(5) A linked internal user named in a contact-typed assigned field (for example `sprk_assignedattorney1`) no longer produces `member_skipped`, and the record appears in their accessible set.
(6) A non-admin cannot edit the two locked fields in MDA. As that same non-admin, Daily Briefing inline to-do creation still defaults Assigned To (the `useInlineTodoCreate.ts:232` read), and the TrackingFieldTrio access modal still shows an internal user as internal (the `index.ts:969` read).
(7) A first sign-in by a user who is linked for the first time shows the linked contact on that same request (no 10-minute wait).
(8) An invite to the email of a workforce-bound dev contact returns 409 with a message and creates no CIAM account.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`Register-EntraAppRegistrations.ps1` configures the `acct` optional claim, and the deployment guide states it as a per-customer requirement. The dev BFF registration carries it. The customer-workforce-tenant setting is in `appsettings.template.json`, in the dev configuration and in the customer deployment guide, including the Model-1 case.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The operator procedure for resolving a collision flag is written in the notes and in `SPAARKE-CUSTOMER-DEPLOYMENT-GUIDE.md`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">SPA-r2 task 023 and its TASK-INDEX row are marked delivered by UAC-r2 141. The A-18 residual comment is removed, and task 013 points to this task.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The BFF unit suite, integration auth tests and NetArchTest are green. The publish-size delta is measured against fresh master per CLAUDE.md §10 (both sizes, the tool and file counts), and the total is at most 60 MB. There is no new HIGH CVE, and the placement decision is stated.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
- Defect C7 (session26-uac-defects-and-synopsis.md:156-178). Owner decisions: session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md round 2 item 4, Q3 (first-sign-in link approved) and the "Live fact" paragraph (only 1 of 8 dev systemusers linked).
